{0 Support and known issues}

Behavior that differs from JavaScript Workers and native OCaml, and features
not supported yet.

{1 Differences from JavaScript Workers}

- Await only in the supported handler contexts. A function that JavaScript
  calls, such as a [setTimeout] callback, cannot await. See
  {{!page-async}Async and background work} for those contexts.
- {!Workero.URL} uses the WHATWG [URL], but {!Workero.URL.path} is decoded and
  only [http] and [https] URLs parse. See
  {{!page-url}URLs}.
- A response cannot have a status below 200 or above 599. Workero raises
  [Invalid_argument] for such a status, also for [`Status 101]. So
  {!Workero.Status} has [`Continue] and [`Switching_Protocols] only to read
  codes; use {!Workero.Websocket} for 101.

{1 Limits of streams, forms and WebSockets}

- A {!Workero.Stream.Writer.write} can reject when the client disconnects.
  {!Workero.Request.signal} aborts earlier for a streamed response, but only
  with the [enable_request_signal] compatibility flag. In [workerd] a handler
  that only awaits a timer got no abort event, and workero has not checked
  Cloudflare production, so do not rely on it to detect every disconnect. See
  {{!page-abort}Abort and timeouts}.
- {!Workero.Response.copy} and {!Workero.Response.of_stream} share the original
  body stream. It can be consumed only once. Call {!Workero.Response.clone}
  before reading if you need two copies.
- {!Workero.Request.form_data} holds the whole upload in memory. See
  {{!page-forms}Forms}.

{1 Limits of abort signals}

- An abort cancels the I/O, not the fiber. OCaml code that computes, or awaits
  something other than a cancelable request, keeps running until it tests
  {!Workero.Abort_signal.aborted} or races {!Workero.Abort_signal.to_promise}.
  {!Workero.Timer.sleep} cannot be canceled.
- [?signal] exists only on {!Workero.fetch}, {!Workero.Fetcher.fetch} and
  {!Workero.Fetcher.fetch_request}. Sockets, WebSockets, KV, R2, D1 and Cache
  take no signal.
- An abort reason is a message: {!Workero.Abort_controller.abort} always gives
  an [AbortError], never another JavaScript value.
- {!Workero.Abort_signal.to_promise} and {!Workero.Abort_signal.on_abort} add a
  listener that stays until the signal aborts. There is no way to remove it, so
  each call on a signal that never aborts keeps one listener for the life of
  the signal.
- {!Workero.Fetcher.fetch_request} with [?signal] replaces the signal that the
  request already has. It does not combine them; use
  {!Workero.Abort_signal.any} for that.

{1 HTML rewriting limits}

- {{!page-html_rewriter}HTML rewriting}: content is a string. A [Response] or
  a stream as content is not wrapped.
- There is no helper that joins the text chunks of a node. Join them until
  {!Workero.Html_rewriter.Text_chunk.last_in_text_node}.
- A handler runs as a fiber, which costs about 1.5 times a plain callback.
  A page with tens of thousands of matches pays about 2 microseconds more for
  each match.
- OCaml exceptions from a handler reach the client only as a truncated body.
- The tests run in [workerd]. They do not prove the order of two handlers that
  wait, because Cloudflare does not document it.

{1 Binding limits}

- {{!page-queue}Queues}: consumers do not receive the content type.
  {!Workero.Queue.Message.body_json} on a [Text] body gives a JSON string.
  The other readers require compatible JavaScript value types and raise
  [Invalid_argument] for incompatible values. A [v8] body can be
  read only through {!Workero.Queue.Message.body_js}; workero producers cannot
  send [v8]. [send] and [send_batch] expose [delay_seconds], but no other send
  options. Cloudflare checks message size; workero does not. Configure dead
  letter queues and [max_retries] in [wrangler.toml].
- {{!page-rate_limiter}Rate limiter}: the key is the only call argument. Set
  the limit and period in [wrangler.toml]. Counts are per location and
  approximate.
- {{!page-ai}Workers AI}: streaming answers and gateway options are not
  supported. {!Workero.Ai.run} raises [Invalid_argument] for a stream.
- {{!page-analytics_engine}Analytics Engine}: [write] returns no result.
  Cloudflare checks the limits of 20 blobs and 20 doubles; workero does not.
  There is no query API.
- {{!page-hyperdrive}Hyperdrive}: connection details only. There is no query
  driver, so workero cannot read from the database yet. The host and password
  are valid only inside the running Worker. In [workerd] the host is a fake name
  with no database behind it.
- {{!page-socket}Sockets}: outbound connections only, from a handler. There is
  no [connect] handler for incoming TCP and no [Socket.upgraded].
  {!Workero.Socket.opened} gives no address information. [workerd] cannot test
  TLS: with a Worker as outbound, [secure_transport:On] and [start_tls] fail.
  Cloudflare limits the destination (no Cloudflare addresses, loopback or
  port 25) and the number of open connections for each request; see its
  documentation.
- Sockets: [index.js] always imports [cloudflare:sockets]. It is available on
  Cloudflare and in [workerd] without a flag. A program run outside a Worker,
  for example in Node.js, raises [Failure] from {!Workero.Socket.connect}.
- {{!page-email}Email}: [workerd] has no way to deliver a test email, and it has
  no [cloudflare:email] module. {!Workero.Email.reply} fails there with a
  {!Workero.Js_error}. The tests call the handler from a JavaScript harness with
  a mock message and a stand-in module, so they do not prove what the real
  runtime accepts.
- Email: {!Workero.Email.reply} takes the raw message as a string, not a stream.
  {!Workero.Email.raw_text} and {!Workero.Email.raw_bytes} copy the whole
  message into the memory of the program; use {!Workero.Email.raw} for a large
  message. The raw message can be read once. The runtime checks the rules for
  [forward] headers and for the [reply] message.
- {{!page-tail}Tail Workers}: only the default export has a [tail] handler,
  registered with {!Workero.tail}. Streaming tails ([tailStream]) are not
  supported. {!type:Workero.Trace.event} has typed cases for fetch, scheduled and
  queue events; any other event is [Other]. [workerd] reports [0.] for
  {!Workero.Trace.wall_time} and {!Workero.Trace.cpu_time}. The tests do not
  cover events other than fetch, scheduled and queue.

{1 Workflow limits}

- {{!page-workflows}Workflows}: workerd has no Workflows engine, so the tests
  run the class and the binding against a small JavaScript engine. It calls the
  OCaml class over RPC as Cloudflare's engine does, and it implements steps,
  retries, [sleep], [wait_for_event] and replay. The tests do not prove
  persistence, eviction, real retry timing, the Cloudflare limits or a
  deployment. Run a Workflow with [wrangler dev] or on Cloudflare before you
  rely on it.
- Step results, event payloads, params and outputs are {!Workero.Rpc.value}s.
  A number above 1073741823 reads as a [`Float], not an [`Int] (see
  {{!page-workflows}Workflows}).
- Not wrapped: [retention], [createBatch] with [count], [deleteBatch],
  [Instance.delete], [subscribe], [terminate] and [restart] options, rollback
  handlers, the rest of the step context ([step], [config]), a function as
  [delay], the cron [schedule] and the [workflowName] of the event.
  [wait_for_event] returns the payload, not the event.
- A Workflow class and a Durable Object or entrypoint class cannot share a name.

{1 Entrypoint limits}

- {{!page-entrypoints}Entrypoints}: the [scheduled], [queue], [email] and [tail]
  handlers of a class are not supported.
- The runtime creates a new instance of an entrypoint for each call, so it keeps
  no state between calls.
- An entrypoint and a Durable Object class cannot share a name.
- A method declared in [~rpc] on a class whose handlers have no [rpc] fails on
  the first call, not at build time.

{1 Durable Object limits}

- The storage API requires a SQLite-backed class. A class listed in
  [new_classes] can build, but its key-value and SQL calls fail at runtime.
  See {{!page-durable_objects}Durable Objects}.
- RPC methods on a stub: names are declared with [register ~rpc]. Arguments and
  results are {!Workero.Rpc.t}. An [Error], a typed array other than
  [Uint8Array], a class instance and a [Symbol] cannot cross the call. A
  [`Function] argument is released when the call returns. A method name
  cannot be [fetch], [alarm], [connect], [constructor], [prototype] or a
  [webSocket*] handler. See
  {{!page-durable_objects}Durable Objects}.
- Stored values are JSON. A [Date] that other code stores reads back as its ISO
  string and a [Map] as [{}]. A [BigInt] or a cyclic value raises
  [Invalid_argument]. {!Workero.Durable_object.Storage.put} raises
  [Invalid_argument] for a NaN or infinite float.
- The function given to [register] cannot call {!Workero.Promise.await}. Load
  stored values in [~init]. Keep per-object state in closures.
- {!Workero.Durable_object.register} must run when the module starts. A
  [register] call that a condition skips is not exported.
- [workero build] needs [workerd]. Set [main] in [wrangler.toml] to the
  [index.js] that [workero build] writes.
- A [block_concurrency_while] function that raises resets the object.
- SQL: [Int] values are exact only within ±[2^53]; a larger integer can
  read back as a [Float]. A whole-number [REAL] such as [2.0] reads back as
  [Int]. [Sql.run] returns only the number of rows written.
  {!Workero.Durable_object.Storage.transaction_sync} rejects
  {!Workero.Promise.await} with [Invalid_argument] and rolls back; use
  {!Workero.Durable_object.Storage.val-transaction} to await.
- {{!page-websockets}WebSockets}: a socket accepted with
  {!Workero.Websocket.accept} does not hibernate. Do not also call
  {!Workero.Durable_object.State.accept_websocket} on that socket. A class
  without [websocket_message] can accept hibernatable sockets, but does not
  handle their messages.
- The [workerd] tests cannot prove eviction and re-creation during hibernation,
  jurisdictions, or the tag and 2048-byte attachment limits. Test these in
  a deployed Worker.
- {{!page-durable_objects}Containers}:
  {!Workero.Durable_object.Container} does not wrap [interceptOutboundTcp],
  [exec], [inspect] or the snapshot functions. The tests run [State.container]
  against [None] in [workerd] and against a JavaScript stub. A separate test,
  [test/worker-containers], starts real containers in CI and prints
  [SKIP containers] where Docker or its images are missing. Raw TCP connect and
  outbound interception run only there. [workerd] runs containers only with
  Docker and an egress interceptor image.

{1 Not supported yet}

- Durable Objects without SQLite storage.
- Vectorize and the Send Email binding.
- Parts of [crypto.subtle]: [deriveKey], wrapping with AES-CBC or AES-CTR,
  [timingSafeEqual], Ed448, RSA public exponents other than 65537, and the key
  properties [algorithm] and [usages]. See {{!page-crypto}Web Crypto}.

{1 Differences from native OCaml}

- [Sys.word_size] is 32, and [int] has 31 bits.
- There is no file system, except files that you embed. See
  {{!page-libraries}Supported libraries}.
- The program has no threads or sub-processes. It can open only outbound TCP
  connections, with {!Workero.Socket}.

Successful compilation does not prove that a library's runtime dependencies
are available in a Worker. See {{!page-libraries}Supported libraries} and
{{!page-testing}Testing a worker}.

{1 Report a problem}

Open an issue in the
{{:https://github.com/ml-in-barcelona/workero}workero repository}. Include the
OCaml, wasm_of_ocaml and Wrangler versions, and the smallest worker that shows
the problem.
